  "body": "The [definition of <code>CanvasFilters.filter</code>](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/scripting.html#filters) says:\n\n> If the value of the filter attribute refers to an SVG filter in an external resource document and that document is not loaded when a drawing operation is invoked, the drawing operation must proceed with no filtering.\n\nOn the other hand, the [CSS definition of filter: url()](https://drafts.fxtf.org/filters/#typedef-filter-url) says:\n\n> If the filter references a non-existent object or the referenced object is not a filter element, then the whole filter chain is ignored. No filter is applied to the object. \n\nIt seems to me that the canvas definition should probably also define the behavior for the referenced object being non-existing, or being an element that is not a filter element.  It could perhaps do this by referring to the CSS definition.\n\nIt might also be good to be more explicit that the behavior is that the entire filter chain is ignored, since \"no filtering\" might be misinterpreted to mean that just the one filter is ignored.\n\n(I also wonder if something in either spec should mention that the load of such an external resource document should be triggered.  This might be more important to mention explicitly on the canvas side.)\n\nI got here via https://github.com/w3ctag/spec-reviews/issues/116 .\n",
